title: 'C-c v l : skg-restrict-repo-set'
pid: a786961b-095d-4165-a41d-518f48ee05e0
body: |2-
  Switches the skgrepo restriction of display and search for this Emacs
  connection. The skgrepo restriction is two things: a skgrepo-set,
  and whether the foreign skgrepos are shown. Skgrepo-sets are the
  prefixes of the config's privacy ladder, the owned skgrepos, so the
  first question the command can answer is really "what is the most
  private owned skgrepo to make available?" -- choosing one activates
  it and every more public owned skgrepo. Foreign skgrepos have no
  place on the ladder; they are shown or hidden all at once, apart from
  the rung, and are shown until you hide them. One prompt offers both:
  "Most private repo to show, or foreign: shown/hidden (S-left/right
  cycle): " lists the owned skgrepos by name in privacy order, the most
  private as "NAME (no restriction)", which is the default, then the two
  choices "foreign: shown" and "foreign: hidden" (only when the config
  has a foreign skgrepo; otherwise the prompt is "Most private repo to
  show (S-left/right cycle): "). Tab completion and shift-left /
  shift-right cycling walk them. Choosing a skgrepo changes the rung and
  leaves the foreign skgrepos as they were; choosing a foreign choice
  shows or hides them and leaves the rung. The command then asks
  "Switch the skgrepo restriction and re-render all SKG buffers? ", and
  on yes tells the server, which uses the new restriction for
  subsequent requests and re-renders every open Skg buffer under it.
  The confirmation reads "Skgrepo restriction: NAME", with "; foreign
  repos hidden" while they are. Choose the most private skgrepo, with
  foreign skgrepos shown, to remove the skgrepo restriction.

  Hidden, a foreign node is treated as any node outside the set, below:
  an open view that showed it re-renders without it.

  Under a skgrepo restriction, a node in a skgrepo outside the set
  shows nothing of itself. Where a relationship recorded in a skgrepo
  the set holds names such a node (say a public node contains a
  private one), the view shows a headline with no title, heralded
  =restricted=, standing for it: a restricted viewnode. It keeps the
  relationship as it was when you save, and deleting its line removes
  the relationship, as deleting an unknown viewnode does;
  =C-c s r= sets its relRepo. A relationship recorded in a skgrepo
  outside the set shows nothing at all, and a save keeps it. The
  skgrepo restriction limits what you can edit as it limits what you can see:
  a save never removes, moves or rewrites a relationship the set keeps
  out, however the buffer looks, and never creates one silently. A save
  that would record a relationship in a skgrepo the set keeps out (a
  restricted viewnode pasted as content, a hide only a relationship
  outside the set explains, =C-c s r= toward such a skgrepo) is refused, naming the
  skgrepo. A fork copies into its clone only the relationships the set
  shows; a node merge, like a delete, is refused when either node has a
  section outside the set; a delete whose cleanup would strip
  relationships outside the set asks first (see the delete). Restoring
  from HEAD, processed requests and the cleanup of an absent ID's
  references are refused unless the connection is unrestricted, and an
  import into a skgrepo outside the set is refused. A
  skgrepo narrowing (a switch to a smaller set) turns an open view's headline
  of such a node into a restricted viewnode in place, its children
  kept, and a skgrepo widening (a switch to a larger set) turns the
  restricted viewnode back. Where nothing the set shows names the node,
  its headline vanishes with what stood under it, and so does a member
  whose record alone the set keeps out (a public node that a private
  record says the headline's parent contains); a skgrepo widening
  draws each again where the graph puts it. A view root is the
  exception: a view rooted at such a node keeps its root, as a
  restricted viewnode, whether or not anything the set shows names it,
  since the view is your own request, and a skgrepo widening makes it
  an occurrence again. The folders under a headline that a
  skgrepo narrowing made a restricted viewnode (its containers folder, say)
  describe a node it shows nothing of, so they become tombstones,
  holding their members, and stay tombstones after the
  skgrepo widening. A buffer of the diff view follows the same rules
  (see C-c v d, 'skg-diffView-open'). The switch reports none of this: it says only
  "Skgrepo restriction: " and the name of the skgrepo restriction.

  The re-render redraws each open buffer from the view the server last
  saved or rendered for it, which would lose an unsaved edit; so while
  any view has unsaved edits the command refuses ("Save or revert every
  skg view before switching the skgrepo restriction"), as stage-and-commit
  does, and asks nothing.

  If the proposed set would rerender overPrivateText telescope text, Skg asks before
  changing the skgrepo restriction or any view. Declining leaves both unchanged and
  an empty rerender stream releases the client's preemptive locks.

  =M-x skg-list-repo-sets= shows the current skgrepo restriction and
  the skgrepo-sets available, and =M-x skg-show-skgrepo-restriction=
  shows the skgrepo restriction alone, each marking the most private
  skgrepo " (no restriction)": "Skgrepo restriction: public; available:
  public, private (no restriction)".
